# The polar divisor of jmath̄ has degree ψ(N)

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.sum_neg_ord_jBar_eq_dedekindPsi`
(`b8399df5-1739-557d-b620-c21ac8c041a9`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `a3e6b6fd-f826-5988-85fd-7af515731c8b`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Fix a natural number $N$ with $N \neq 0$. Work inside the field
$\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$, where $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`,
and let $\overline{\mathbb Q}F_N =$ [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldBar
N`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L111) be the intermediate field obtained by adjoining to
$\overline{\mathbb Q}$ the coefficientwise images, under the map `coeffEmb` induced by $\mathbb Q
\hookrightarrow \overline{\mathbb Q}$, of the field $\mathbb Q$-generated by the divisor expansions
attached to $N$; let $\bar\jmath =$ [`ModularCurve.jBar
N`](def/ModularCurve_MazurStepThreeInputs.html#L43) be the element of this field given by the
coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion `jq` of the modular invariant. Places of
$\overline{\mathbb Q}F_N$ over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ are, in the project's sense, valuation
subrings of $\overline{\mathbb Q}F_N$ that contain the image of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, are proper,
and are principal ideal rings. The data are a finite set $S$ of such places together with the
hypothesis that a place $v$ lies in $S$ precisely when $\mathrm{ord}_v(\bar\jmath) < 0$, i.e. $S$
enumerates exactly the poles of $\bar\jmath$. The conclusion is the integer identity $\sum_{v \in S}
\bigl(-\mathrm{ord}_v(\bar\jmath)\bigr) = \psi(N)$, where $\psi(N) =$ [`ModularCurve.dedekindPsi
N`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L201) is defined as $\sum_{d \mid N,\ d \text{ squarefree}} N/d$.

This is the statement that the polar divisor of the modular invariant on $X_0(N)$ over
$\overline{\mathbb Q}$ — supported at the cusps — has degree $\psi(N) = [\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z) :
\Gamma_0(N)]$, the degree of the map $\bar\jmath$. It is the companion, for poles, of the result
computing the degree of the divisor of zeros of $\bar\jmath - j_0$, and it feeds the Riemann–Roch
and genus computations for $\overline{\mathbb Q}F_N$ used further on.

